{
  "case_id": "2007-06-989",
  "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Phillips, would you say the same thing if the agreement provided for de novo review in the district court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first today in Case 06-989, Hall Street Associates v. Mattel, Inc."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Indeed, are there not such things as nonbinding arbitration's?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: It's not that you don't need Section 9. You want to get rid of Section 9 --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Because under Section 2 the parties have -- have provided a mechanism for that by saying that the district court will correct an award if it's erroneous as a matter of law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: That is, indeed, the issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF BETH S. BRINKMANN ON BEHALF OF RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: That's a strange argument in this respect. You are arguing that this non-Article III person has more control rather than less control; that if the judge controlled this arbitrator, somehow that would violate Article III. But if the judge has no control and is essentially little more than a rubber stamp on what the non-Article III person does, then that's all right."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Brinkmann",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BRINKMANN: Well, Your Honor, we are not suggesting that it's the only means to get an award enforced, but if you are doing the Section 9 route, the grounds in the statute are the only grounds on which that can be done, and the policy about whether or not those transaction costs, when parties want further review on an arbitration, is shifted to the courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why couldn't you limit it reasonably by saying the parties can agree to anything?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amici",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0003-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I think that the amici really point out that that is so contrary to the finality and efficiencies that the animal of arbitration --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Brinkmann",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "50300a32c43e5adb254d0a93c68992d52416b36348f56c8fce834a427e05aec3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-50300a32c43e-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BRINKMANN: No, but you're putting -- you are, I think, as this Court itself has said, you're breeding litigation from a statute whose whole point was to minimize and limit litigation. You're creating a new body of Federal common law that's really antithetical to the core purpose of the Arbitration Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-989",
      "confidence": 0.0,
